</table>
</span></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering" style="font-size:80%;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 3: </span>
<span id="S5.T3.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">OR-GIGPO, model scale, and tool access all affect performance.</span>
OR-GIGPO gives the best 8B result among RL algorithms, and the same recipe improves the 2B and 4B backbones.
The full tool sandbox also outperforms no-tool, previous-tool, and tool-family removal settings, showing that perception and interaction tools are complementary. All scores are accuracy percentages.
</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S5.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Observation-relaxed credit assignment improves multi-step tool learning.</span> Table <a href="#S5.T3" title="Table 3 ‣ 5.2 Ablation Studies ‣ 5 Experiments ‣ Perceive, Interact, Reason: Building Tool-Augmented Visual Agents for Spatial Reasoning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> compares OR-GIGPO with original GIGPO, GRPO, and DAPO on in-distribution benchmarks.
OR-GIGPO achieves the best average accuracy, outperforming GIGPO by <math id="S5.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2.8" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2.8</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2.8</annotation></semantics></math>, GRPO by <math id="S5.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2.4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2.4</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2.4</annotation></semantics></math>, and DAPO by <math id="S5.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="11.1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>11.1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">11.1</annotation></semantics></math> percentage points, which supports the value of observation-relaxed step-level advantages for fine-grained credit assignment in multi-step tool use.
The <span id="S5.SS2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Average Step Adv Ratio</span> measures the fraction of trajectories with any nonzero step advantage: <math id="S5.SS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="9.3\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>9.3</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">9.3\%</annotation></semantics></math> for GIGPO and <math id="S5.SS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="62.3\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>62.3</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">62.3\%</annotation></semantics></math> for OR-GIGPO.
This indicates that, in the setting of visual tool agents, original GIGPO struggles to match exactly identical observations and therefore largely degrades to GRPO-level behavior, while OR-GIGPO constructs relaxed step-level outcome groups that enable fine-grained advantage assignment.
The similarity threshold must remain selective: lowering it from <math id="S5.SS2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta=0.9" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.9</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta=0.9</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S5.SS2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta=0.5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta=0.5</annotation></semantics></math> further raises the step advantage ratio to <math id="S5.SS2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="68.5\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>68.5</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">68.5\%</annotation></semantics></math> but reduces the average accuracy to <math id="S5.SS2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="48.9\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>48.9</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">48.9\%</annotation></semantics></math>, as dissimilar observations are merged into the same outcome group.
DAPO performs worse than the baseline, suggesting that its algorithmic design may not be well suited to our visual tool-use setting, where early-stage tool-calling ability is still unstable.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold ltx_font_smallcaps">PERIA<span id="S5.SS2.p2.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_upright">’s training recipe is effective beyond the 8B model.</span></span> Table <a href="#S5.T3" title="Table 3 ‣ 5.2 Ablation Studies ‣ 5 Experiments ‣ Perceive, Interact, Reason: Building Tool-Augmented Visual Agents for Spatial Reasoning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> also shows that the proposed training recipe transfers to smaller backbones.
Compared with their Qwen3 baselines, <span id="S5.SS2.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">PERIA</span>-2B improves from <math id="S5.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="35.9\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>35.9</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">35.9\%</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S5.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="39.3\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>39.3</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">39.3\%</annotation></semantics></math>, and <span id="S5.SS2.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">PERIA</span>-4B improves from <math id="S5.SS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="41.2\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>41.2</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">41.2\%</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S5.SS2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="48.8\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>48.8</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">48.8\%</annotation></semantics></math> on average.
This indicates the generality of <span id="S5.SS2.p2.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">PERIA</span> across model scales.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Both perception and interaction tools are needed for robust spatial reasoning.</span> Table <a href="#S5.T3" title="Table 3 ‣ 5.2 Ablation Studies ‣ 5 Experiments ‣ Perceive, Interact, Reason: Building Tool-Augmented Visual Agents for Spatial Reasoning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> evaluates <span id="S5.SS2.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">PERIA</span>-8B under different inference-time tool conditions.
Removing all tools reduces the average score to <math id="S5.SS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="44.4\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>44.4</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">44.4\%</annotation></semantics></math>, showing that tool access is necessary for robust performance across diverse spatial reasoning domains.
Using only the previous tool set reaches <math id="S5.SS2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="47.6\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>47.6</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">47.6\%</annotation></semantics></math>, clearly below the full sandbox; this previous set contains six tools adopted from existing work: <span id="S5.SS2.p3.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">image_crop</span>, <span id="S5.SS2.p3.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">bounding_box</span>, <span id="S5.SS2.p3.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">image_highlight</span>, <span id="S5.SS2.p3.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">text_ocr</span>, <span id="S5.SS2.p3.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">auto_segment</span>, and <span id="S5.SS2.p3.1.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">grounding_dino</span>.
This gap demonstrates that our <span id="S5.SS2.p3.1.9" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">PERIA</span> tool design is more effective than generic visual utilities for spatial reasoning.
Removing either perception or interaction tools also degrades performance, confirming their complementary roles in evidence acquisition, local verification, and spatial planning.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS2.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Additional ablations and analyses.</span> We provide further details and studies in the appendix.
Appendix <a href="#A2.SS1.SSS0.Px5" title="Interaction cap. ‣ B.1 Training and Inference ‣ Appendix B Implementation Details ‣ Perceive, Interact, Reason: Building Tool-Augmented Visual Agents for Spatial Reasoning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B.1</span></a> reports the interaction-cap diagnostic, followed by detailed tool definitions (Appendix <a href="#A3.SS1" title="C.1 Tool Sandbox ‣ Appendix C Method Details ‣ Perceive, Interact, Reason: Building Tool-Augmented Visual Agents for Spatial Reasoning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">C.1</span></a>) and the cost of thought diversification (Appendix <a href="#A3.SS2.SSS0.Px4" title="Diversification cost. ‣ C.2 Trajectory Synthesis ‣ Appendix C Method Details ‣ Perceive, Interact, Reason: Building Tool-Augmented Visual Agents for Spatial Reasoning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">C.2</span></a>).
Appendix <a href="#A4.SS3" title="D.3 MapTrace Evaluation Criterion Ablation ‣ Appendix D Dataset Details ‣ Perceive, Interact, Reason: Building Tool-Augmented Visual Agents for Spatial Reasoning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">D.3</span></a> examines the MapTrace evaluation criterion.
Appendix <a href="#A5" title="Appendix E Additional Experiments ‣ Perceive, Interact, Reason: Building Tool-Augmented Visual Agents for Spatial Reasoning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">E</span></a> studies trajectory-teacher quality (Appendix <a href="#A5.SS1" title="E.1 Teacher Quality ‣ Appendix E Additional Experiments ‣ Perceive, Interact, Reason: Building Tool-Augmented Visual Agents for Spatial Reasoning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">E.1</span></a>) and the rollout group size of OR-GIGPO (Appendix <a href="#A5.SS2" title="E.2 Rollout Group Size ‣ Appendix E Additional Experiments ‣ Perceive, Interact, Reason: Building Tool-Augmented Visual Agents for Spatial Reasoning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">E.2</span></a>).
Appendix <a href="#A6" title="Appendix F Fine-grained Tool-use Analysis ‣ Perceive, Interact, Reason: Building Tool-Augmented Visual Agents for Spatial Reasoning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">F</span></a> then presents fine-grained tool-use analyses: tool-call distribution (Appendix <a href="#A6.SS1" title="F.1 Tool-use Distribution ‣ Appendix F Fine-grained Tool-use Analysis ‣ Perceive, Interact, Reason: Building Tool-Augmented Visual Agents for Spatial Reasoning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">F.1</span></a>), zero-shot transfer of the <span id="S5.SS2.p4.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">PERIA</span> sandbox to prior agents (Appendix <a href="#A6.SS2" title="F.2 Zero-shot Interface Transfer ‣ Appendix F Fine-grained Tool-use Analysis ‣ Perceive, Interact, Reason: Building Tool-Augmented Visual Agents for Spatial Reasoning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">F.2</span></a>), map-oriented-tool removal and map/non-map balance (Appendix <a href="#A6.SS3" title="F.3 Map-oriented Tools and Map/Non-map Balance ‣ Appendix F Fine-grained Tool-use Analysis ‣ Perceive, Interact, Reason: Building Tool-Augmented Visual Agents for Spatial Reasoning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">F.3</span></a>), and tool effectiveness on Cube Three-View (Appendix <a href="#A6.SS4" title="F.4 Tool Effectiveness on Cube Three-View ‣ Appendix F Fine-grained Tool-use Analysis ‣ Perceive, Interact, Reason: Building Tool-Augmented Visual Agents for Spatial Reasoning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">F.4</span></a>).</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.3 </span>Qualitative Analysis</h3>

<figure id="S5.F4" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2606.12830v2/qualitative_result.png" id="S5.F4.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/180;" width="476" height="180" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 4: </span>
<span id="S5.F4.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold ltx_font_smallcaps">PERIA<span id="S5.F4.4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_upright"> grounds route tracing with visual tools.</span></span>
In this qualitative example, <span id="S5.F4.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">PERIA</span> uses perception tools to identify station labels, interaction tools to verify the relevant region, and accumulated observations to trace the route and answer correctly.
</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S5.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Figure <a href="#S5.F4" title="Figure 4 ‣ 5.3 Qualitative Analysis ‣ 5 Experiments ‣ Perceive, Interact, Reason: Building Tool-Augmented Visual Agents for Spatial Reasoning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> illustrates how <span id="S5.SS3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">PERIA</span> combines perception and interaction for subway-map reasoning.
Counting intermediate stops between Grand Av Arts/Bunker Hill and Jefferson/USC requires recovering the connecting station sequence and excluding the endpoints.
Small labels and intersecting lines make this difficult to verify from the full map alone.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS3.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Perception identifies relevant entities; interaction verifies their spatial relationships.</span>
<span id="S5.SS3.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">PERIA</span> first invokes <span id="S5.SS3.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">map_text_ocr</span> to extract station labels.
The observation contains relevant names but lacks a sufficiently structured sequence for reliable counting.
The agent therefore invokes <span id="S5.SS3.p2.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">image_crop</span> to inspect the queried region and verify the local route.
It identifies LATTC/Ortho Institute, Pico, and 7th St/Metro Ctr as the three intermediate stops and returns <math id="S5.SS3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>3</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3</annotation></semantics></math>.
The tools play complementary roles: perception narrows the search, while interaction resolves local spatial uncertainty.
The answer follows from reasoning over both observations rather than accepting the initial OCR output as a complete solution.
More examples appear in Appendix <a href="#A7" title="Appendix G Qualitative Examples ‣ Perceive, Interact, Reason: Building Tool-Augmented Visual Agents for Spatial Reasoning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">G</span></a>.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S6" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">6 </span>Conclusion</h2>

<div id="S6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We presented <span id="S6.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">PERception-Interaction-reason Agent</span> (<span id="S6.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold ltx_font_smallcaps">PERIA</span>), a tool-augmented visual agent framework for general-purpose spatial reasoning. <span id="S6.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">PERIA</span> integrates perception and interaction tools into a unified Perceive–Interact–Reason process, enabling agents to acquire global spatial evidence, perform fine-grained local verification, and reason over accumulated observations. To train such agents, we introduced supervised tool-use trajectory synthesis and OR-GIGPO, an observation-relaxed policy optimization method for multi-step visual tool use. Experimental results show that <span id="S6.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">PERIA</span> consistently improves spatial reasoning performance, approaches much larger proprietary and open-source models, and benefits from both the designed tool sandbox and the two-stage training recipe. These results suggest that trained visual tool use is a promising direction for building more capable spatial reasoning agents. We discuss limitations and future work in Appendix <a href="#A1" title="Appendix A Limitations and Future Work ‣ Perceive, Interact, Reason: Building Tool-Augmented Visual Agents for Spatial Reasoning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<section id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Broader impact.</h4>

<div id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">PERIA</span> co-designs a heterogeneous spatial tool sandbox, executable trajectory supervision, and observation-relaxed credit assignment, making multi-tool spatial reasoning agents trainable.
Our findings suggest that tool access alone does not yield tool competence, that executable and verified trajectories are needed to establish it, and that credit assignment should tolerate the surface-form variation of tool observations.
We therefore expect future tool-augmented multimodal agents to benefit from designing the tool interface, trajectory supervision, and credit assignment jointly rather than in isolation.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Such agents may support visual assistance, map understanding, robotics, and navigation, but incorrect tool-grounded reasoning may mislead safety-sensitive decisions and the framework could be misused for surveillance; responsible deployment should include task-specific evaluation and human oversight.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
