<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering" style="font-size:80%;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 9: </span>
<span id="A4.T9.9" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">MapTrace evaluation under different NDTW thresholds.</span>
We report threshold-based accuracy under three NDTW thresholds and the average NDTW distance.
Higher is better for threshold-based accuracy, while lower is better for Avg. NDTW. Accuracy gains are percentage points; NDTW changes are distance differences.
<span id="A4.T9.10" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">PERIA</span>-8B improves over the Qwen3-8B backbone across all threshold settings and achieves the best accuracy among all models under the strictest threshold. Bold and underlined values indicate the best and second-best results across all listed models, respectively.
</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="A4.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A4.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We use the NDTW path distance from MapTrace <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">Panagopoulou et al., 2025</a>]</cite>, but summarize it as threshold-based accuracy rather than Avg. NDTW: for a threshold <math id="A4.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math>, we report the fraction of test examples with distance below <math id="A4.SS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math>. The main results use <math id="A4.SS3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau=1.0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1.0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau=1.0</annotation></semantics></math>, while Table <a href="#A4.T9" title="Table 9 ‣ D.3 MapTrace Evaluation Criterion Ablation ‣ Appendix D Dataset Details ‣ Perceive, Interact, Reason: Building Tool-Augmented Visual Agents for Spatial Reasoning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a> also reports <math id="A4.SS3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau=0.5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau=0.5</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A4.SS3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.3</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.3</annotation></semantics></math>.
As shown in Table <a href="#A4.T9" title="Table 9 ‣ D.3 MapTrace Evaluation Criterion Ablation ‣ Appendix D Dataset Details ‣ Perceive, Interact, Reason: Building Tool-Augmented Visual Agents for Spatial Reasoning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a>, Avg. NDTW is sensitive to extreme values and may not reliably reflect the capability of different models.
We therefore also report model performance under stricter thresholds.
<span id="A4.SS3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">PERIA</span>-8B achieves the highest threshold-based accuracy among non-commercial models at all three thresholds, with a larger gain over its backbone under the strictest criterion.
At the threshold of <math id="A4.SS3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.3</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.3</annotation></semantics></math>, <span id="A4.SS3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">PERIA</span>-8B achieves the best result among all models.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A5" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix E </span>Additional Experiments</h2>

<div id="A5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This appendix reports additional experiments on the training recipe of <span id="A5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">PERIA</span>. Appendix <a href="#A5.SS1" title="E.1 Teacher Quality ‣ Appendix E Additional Experiments ‣ Perceive, Interact, Reason: Building Tool-Augmented Visual Agents for Spatial Reasoning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">E.1</span></a> examines how the quality of the trajectory teacher affects SFT, and Appendix <a href="#A5.SS2" title="E.2 Rollout Group Size ‣ Appendix E Additional Experiments ‣ Perceive, Interact, Reason: Building Tool-Augmented Visual Agents for Spatial Reasoning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">E.2</span></a> studies the rollout group size of OR-GIGPO.</p>
</div>
<section id="A5.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">E.1 </span>Teacher Quality</h3>

<div id="A5.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A5.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We replaced the GPT-5 trajectory teacher with the open-weight Qwen3.5-397B-A17B teacher while keeping the diversification model, SFT budget, and evaluation protocol fixed. Each SFT-only checkpoint was evaluated once on the same stratified 10% sample from all 13 benchmarks. Table <a href="#A5.T10" title="Table 10 ‣ E.1 Teacher Quality ‣ Appendix E Additional Experiments ‣ Perceive, Interact, Reason: Building Tool-Augmented Visual Agents for Spatial Reasoning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10</span></a> shows that the open-weight teacher already improves over the no-SFT checkpoint, while GPT-5 provides a further gain; this is a diagnostic of teacher quality rather than a replacement for the full-test-set main comparison.</p>
</div>
<figure id="A5.T10" class="ltx_table">
<table id="A5.T10.2" class="ltx_tabular ltx_centering ltx_align_middle">
<tr id="A5.T10.2.1" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="A5.T10.2.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_tt" style="padding-left:5.0pt;padding-right:5.0pt;"><span id="A5.T10.2.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;">SFT setting</span></td>
<td id="A5.T10.2.1.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_tt" style="padding-left:5.0pt;padding-right:5.0pt;"><span id="A5.T10.2.1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;">Average accuracy</span></td></tr>
<tr id="A5.T10.2.2" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="A5.T10.2.2.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_t" style="padding-left:5.0pt;padding-right:5.0pt;"><span id="A5.T10.2.2.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Qwen3-VL-8B, no SFT</span></td>
<td id="A5.T10.2.2.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t" style="padding-left:5.0pt;padding-right:5.0pt;"><span id="A5.T10.2.2.2.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">34.1</span></td></tr>
<tr id="A5.T10.2.3" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="A5.T10.2.3.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left" style="padding-left:5.0pt;padding-right:5.0pt;"><span id="A5.T10.2.3.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Qwen3.5-397B-A17B teacher</span></td>
<td id="A5.T10.2.3.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center" style="padding-left:5.0pt;padding-right:5.0pt;"><span id="A5.T10.2.3.2.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">37.6</span></td></tr>
<tr id="A5.T10.2.4" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="A5.T10.2.4.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_bb" style="padding-left:5.0pt;padding-right:5.0pt;"><span id="A5.T10.2.4.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">GPT-5 teacher</span></td>
<td id="A5.T10.2.4.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb" style="padding-left:5.0pt;padding-right:5.0pt;"><span id="A5.T10.2.4.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;">39.8</span></td></tr>
</table>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering" style="font-size:90%;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 10: </span>Teacher-quality diagnostic on the same stratified 10% sample from all 13 benchmarks. The open-weight teacher improves over the no-SFT checkpoint, while GPT-5 provides a further gain in this diagnostic.</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="A5.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">E.2 </span>Rollout Group Size</h3>

<div id="A5.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A5.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For the rollout-group-size diagnostic, we evaluated Qwen3-VL-2B-Thinking with a fixed 10% sample from each of the six in-distribution benchmarks, shared by all settings in this comparison. As shown in Table <a href="#A5.T11" title="Table 11 ‣ E.2 Rollout Group Size ‣ Appendix E Additional Experiments ‣ Perceive, Interact, Reason: Building Tool-Augmented Visual Agents for Spatial Reasoning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">11</span></a>, increasing the group size from <math id="A5.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N=4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N=4</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="A5.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N=8" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>8</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N=8</annotation></semantics></math> yields a further improvement within the tested range.</p>
</div>
<figure id="A5.T11" class="ltx_table">
<table id="A5.T11.2" class="ltx_tabular ltx_centering ltx_align_middle">
<tr id="A5.T11.2.1" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="A5.T11.2.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_tt" style="padding-left:5.0pt;padding-right:5.0pt;"><span id="A5.T11.2.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;">Setting</span></td>
<td id="A5.T11.2.1.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_tt" style="padding-left:5.0pt;padding-right:5.0pt;"><span id="A5.T11.2.1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;">Average accuracy</span></td>
